    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can u go a bit deeper into the cellular level stuff? like what actually happens once leucine hits that mTOR signal? also i've heard people talk about \"metabolic efficiency\" in this context, does a surplus ever cause issues with how the body uses that nitrogen?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Once leucine binds to the Rag GTPases on the lysosomal surface, it triggers a conformational change that allows mTORC1 to recruit and activate effector proteins like S6K1 and 4E-BP1, initiating cap-dependent translation. Regarding metabolic efficiency, while a surplus prevents nitrogen loss, it can lead to protein wasting if amino acids exceed catabolic needs; the body relies on muscle glycogen or gluconeogenesis rather than breaking down its own protein to extract glucose from that excess nitrogen-rich input. Consequently, without adequate energy intake to burn off that surplus nitrogen, your body may lose about 20-30% of it to uric acid production, meaning the remainder is profitable for muscle growth only if you are truly energy positive or performing hypertrophic resistance training.",
      "source": "target",
